' F4 -- Actors and goals.
' Chapter 3 (Requirements). UML use case diagram.
' Claim supported: three roles with asymmetric capabilities; content authoring
' is gated on OWNERSHIP, not role, which is a deliberate design decision with
' a security consequence (see F14).
@startuml f4-use-case
!include _style.iuml

title Actors and goals (as implemented)

left to right direction

actor "Student" as S
actor "Professor" as P
actor "Admin" as A

rectangle "Learnstation" {
  usecase "Read lecture\n+ structured notes" as U1
  usecase "Ask the grounded\nAI tutor" as U2
  usecase "Answer in-lecture\nquizzes" as U3
  usecase "Review queue (SRS)" as U4 #DCDCDC
  usecase "Take a mock exam" as U5 #DCDCDC
  usecase "Upload own\nmaterials" as U6 #DCDCDC

  usecase "Upload + edit\na lecture" as U7
  usecase "Review AI-extracted\ncontent (batch)" as U8
  usecase "Manage courses" as U9
  usecase "View analytics" as U10

  usecase "Grant / revoke roles" as U11
  usecase "Platform stats,\nLLM spend" as U12
  usecase "Backups, analytics reset" as U13
}

S --> U1
S --> U2
S --> U3
S --> U4
S --> U5
S --> U6

P --> U7
P --> U8
P --> U9
P --> U10
P --> U1
P --> U2

A --> U11
A --> U12
A --> U13

note bottom
  **Shaded use cases are unreachable in a default deployment.**
  Every feature flag in the project defaults to off, so the review queue,
  mock exams and student uploads ship dark. See F17.

  **Authoring is gated on ownership, not role.** `require_creator` admits
  both professor and student (auth_middleware.py:243) — the comment states
  the intent: "ownership (not role) is what RLS and endpoint checks gate on."
  Consequence: a student-uploaded PDF can reach another student's tutor
  context, which is why retrieved text is treated as untrusted. See F14.
end note

legend bottom
  Professors reach "Read lecture" and the tutor by URL only — their navigation
  chrome has no entry for either (ConsoleTopBar.tsx:36-42).
endlegend

@enduml
